\b\fs24 Introduction\b0\fs22\par
Welcome to the Quote Conversion Report Automation program! This tool is designed to streamline and automate the process of generating, processing, and distributing various quote conversion reports. It aims to reduce manual effort, improve consistency, and provide timely information.\par
The program offers both manual control via a user interface and powerful automated capabilities for scheduled report generation.\par
\par
\b\fs24 How the Application Works (Overview)\b0\fs22\par
The application orchestrates several components:\par
\par
User Interface (UI): Allows for manual selection of report types, date ranges, and processing options. It also provides access to configuration settings and logs.\par
\par
Crystal Report Wrapper: An external service used to extract raw report data from the primary business system (via Crystal Reports).\par
\par
Excel Processing: Utilises templates to process the raw data into final, formatted Excel reports. This includes data cleaning, calculations, and potentially filtering (e.g., for the 'Daily 5-days >= \'a31k' report).\par
\par
Email Distribution: Sends the final reports to configured recipients via SMTP.\par
\par
Automation Engine: Handles scheduled, automated generation and emailing of predefined reports based on configuration.\par
\par
Configuration Files: Uses appsettings.json for main settings and user-specific JSON files (in your AppData folder) for customisations like email recipients, greetings, and bank holidays.\par
\par
How to Use the Application (Manual Operation)\par
Main Interface Elements:\par
Report Type: Dropdown to select the desired report period (e.g., Daily, Weekly).\par
\par
From / To Dates: Date pickers for defining the report range. These are often auto-filled based on the Report Type.\par
\par
Financial Year: Dropdown (visible for certain report types like Weekly or Custom) to specify the financial year context, primarily for Power BI source file updates.\par
\par
Report Settings Group:\par
\par
Send to only Femi?: (Visible for non-Daily, non-Custom manual reports) Restricts email distribution to a specific IT/admin list.\par
\par
Skip Sending Email: If ticked, the program will generate and process the report files locally but will not send an email.\par
\par
Action Buttons: Used to generate and process reports. The appearance depends on the '1-Click Processing' mode (see Options Menu):\par
\par
Standard Mode (2-button): Create Report (generates raw data) then Process & Email Report (processes raw data and emails).\par
\par
1-Click Mode: A single Generate, Process & Email Report button performs all steps.\par
\par
View Buttons: Appear after successful generation: View Raw Report and View Processed Analysis.\par
\par
Enable/Disable Daily Auto Run @ %%CURRENT_AUTORUN_HOUR%%:00: Toggles the automated daily report generation feature.\par
\par
Status Bar: Displays the current status of operations (left) and auto-run status (right).\par
\par
Step-by-Step Guide for Manual Report Generation:\par
1. Select Report Type:\par
Choose from the Report Type dropdown. Dates will often adjust automatically. Bank holidays (England & Wales, plus custom) are considered for 'previous working day' calculations.\par
\par
Daily: Report for the previous working day.\par
\par
Daily (5days >= \'a31000): Covers the previous five working days. After raw data generation, it filters for estimates with a 'Net Value' of \'a31000 or more before final analysis.\par
\par
Weekly: Covers a 15-day rolling period ending on the current day. Data is appended to a central Excel file used by Power BI.\par
\par
Monthly: Report for the previous full calendar month.\par
\par
Quarterly: Report for the previous full calendar quarter.\par
\par
Annual: Report for the previous full financial year (1st May - 30th April).\par
\par
Custom: Allows manual selection of 'From' and 'To' dates. If you change dates for any other report type, it will automatically switch to 'Custom'.\par
\par
2. Adjust Dates (Optional):\par
If 'Custom' is selected, or if you wish to override the auto-calculated dates for other types, use the From Date and To Date pickers. Changing dates will set the Report Type to 'Custom'.\par
\par
3. Select Financial Year (If Applicable):\par
For 'Weekly' or 'Custom' reports, the Financial Year dropdown may be visible. Select the appropriate financial year if the report data needs to be associated with a specific year for Power BI updates or analysis.\par
\par
4. Configure Report Settings:\par
\par
Tick Send to only Femi?: if you want to restrict the email distribution to a predefined IT/admin list. This is typically used for testing or specific non-standard reports.\par
\par
Tick Skip Sending Email if you only want to generate the report files locally and do not wish for an email to be sent.\par
\par
5. Process the Report:\par
\par
If using Standard Mode, first click Create Report. Wait for the status to indicate completion. Then, click Process & Email Report.\par
\par
If using 1-Click Mode (see Options Menu to enable), click the single Generate, Process & Email Report button.\par
\par
6. Manual Excel Refresh (for Monthly, Quarterly, Annual, Custom reports):\par
For these report types, the Excel template contains PivotTables that require manual refreshing after the data is populated. You will be prompted:\par
\par
The Excel file will open automatically.\par
\par
If prompted by Excel, click 'Enable Editing'.\par
\par
Go to the 'Data' tab in Excel and click 'Refresh All'.\par
\par
Specifically, ensure PivotTables on the 'OrderPivot' and 'Estimate Success PivotTable' sheets are updated. You may need to right-click them and select 'Refresh'. Check any Slicers as well.\par
\par
SAVE the Excel file.\par
\par
CLOSE Excel.\par
\par
The application will then ask you to confirm if you want to proceed with emailing the (now refreshed) report.\par
\par
7. Viewing Reports:\par
\par
After the raw report is generated, the View Raw Report button becomes active. Click it to open the raw Excel data file.\par
\par
After the report is processed (and emailed, if not skipped), the View Processed Analysis button becomes active. Click it to open the final, formatted Excel report.\par
\par
Options Menu Explained\par
The 'Options' menu provides access to various settings and tools:\par
\par
Enable 1-Click Processing: Toggles between the standard two-button mode ('Create Report' then 'Process & Email') and a single 'Generate, Process & Email Report' button.\par
\par
Set Auto-Run Hour...: Allows you to change the hour (0-23) for the daily automated report check. The current setting is approximately %%CURRENT_AUTORUN_HOUR%%:00.\par
\par
Configure Auto-Run Reports: A sub-menu to individually enable or disable specific automated reports:\par
\par
Enable Standard Daily Auto Report\par
\par
Enable Daily (5days >= \'a31000) Auto Report\par
\par
Enable Weekly Auto Report\par
\par
Dark Mode: Toggles the application's visual theme between light and dark mode.\par
\par
View Configuration: Displays a summary of critical file paths and settings used by the application, helping to diagnose configuration issues.\par
\par
Validate Configuration: Performs a quick check of essential configurations (e.g., Crystal Report path, Wrapper EXE path) and updates the status bar.\par
\par
Manage Custom Bank Holidays: Opens a window to add, view, or remove custom one-off or recurring bank holidays. These are used in 'previous working day' calculations.\par
\par
Manage Email Recipients: Opens a window to customise the 'To' and 'CC' email lists for different report scenarios (automated, manual, debug). User changes are saved and override application defaults.\par
\par
Manage Email Greetings: Opens a window to customise the introductory greeting text for emails in different scenarios. User changes are saved and override application defaults.\par
\par
Open Logs Folder: Opens the directory where the application's detailed log files are stored. This is crucial for troubleshooting.\par
\par
Edit appsettings.json: Opens the main configuration file (appsettings.json) in the default text editor. Use with extreme caution! Incorrect changes can break the application.\par
\par
Exit: Closes the application.\par
\par
Automated Features\par
Auto-Run Feature:\par
When the Enable Daily Auto Run @ %%CURRENT_AUTORUN_HOUR%%:00 button shows green (enabled), the application will automatically check around %%CURRENT_AUTORUN_HOUR%%:00 each day to run any pending automated reports.\par
The following reports can be configured for auto-run via the Options -> Configure Auto-Run Reports menu:\par
\par
Standard Daily Report\par
\par
Daily (5days >= \'a31000) Report\par
\par
Weekly Report (typically runs on a specific day like Friday)\par
Automated reports use email recipients and greetings configured for their specific auto-run scenario (see 'Manage Email Recipients' and 'Manage Email Greetings'). The status of the auto-run process is displayed in the right-hand side of the status bar.\par
The application keeps track of which reports have successfully run for the day in appsettings.json to avoid duplicate runs if the application is restarted.\par
\par
Automated Archiving:\par
The application performs automated archiving on startup:\par
\par
Log Files: Log files older than 30 days are moved to an 'Archive' subfolder within your user-specific log directory, structured by Year, Month, and Week number (e.g., Logs\\[YourUser]\\Archive\\YYYY\\MM\\WeekN).\par
\par
Report Files:\par
\par
Final Reports (e.g., in 'Estimates' folder): Folders representing previous calendar years (e.g., a '2023' folder within 'Weekly Reports') are moved into a main 'Archive' folder at the root of the final reports directory (e.g., Estimates\\Archive\\Weekly Reports\\2023). If the target archive year folder already exists, contents are merged.\par
\par
Raw Reports (e.g., in 'Estimate Reports Exports' folder): Individual raw report files (.xlsx) older than a configured number of days (default 30) are moved into an 'Archive\\YYYY-MM' subfolder within their respective report type directory (e.g., Estimate Reports Exports\\Daily Reports\\Archive\\YYYY-MM\\OldFile.xlsx).\par
\par
Configuration Files Overview\par
The application uses several configuration files:\par
\par
appsettings.json: The main configuration file located with the application. It stores critical paths (Crystal Report, Wrapper EXE, Template base), SMTP server details, default email recipients and greetings, auto-run settings (like LastRunDate, DailyRunStatus, enabled reports), and logging levels. Modifying this file directly requires caution.\par
\par
User-Specific JSON Files: These are stored in your user profile's AppData directory (typically %APPDATA%\\HarlowSolutions\\QuoteConversionReportAutomation\\). They allow for personal customisations without altering the main appsettings.json:\par
\par
user_email_settings.json: Stores your custom email recipient lists, overriding the defaults from appsettings.json.\par
\par
user_greeting_settings.json: Stores your custom email greetings.\par
\par
custom_bank_holidays.json: Stores any custom bank holidays you have defined.\par
\par
Troubleshooting\par
If you encounter issues, consider the following steps:\par
\par
"Config Error" Status: This usually indicates a problem with essential file paths.\par
\par
Go to Options -> View Configuration to check the paths for the Crystal Report file (.rpt), the Crystal Report Wrapper executable (.exe), and the Excel Template base directory.\par
\par
Ensure all listed files and folders exist and are accessible from your machine. The application may require network access to some of these paths.\par
\par
Report Generation Fails (Raw Report):\par
\par
Ensure the Crystal Report Wrapper service (CrystalReportWrapper.exe) is running or can be started by the application.\par
\par
Verify the Crystal Report file path in appsettings.json (viewable via Options -> View Configuration) is correct and the file is accessible.\par
\par
Check the application logs for specific error messages from the wrapper service (see 'Open Logs Folder' below).\par
\par
Excel Processing Fails (Analysis Report):\par
\par
Ensure the Excel template files (e.g., TEMPLATE_Estimate Success Rate.xlsx) exist in the configured 'TEMPLATE' directory and are not corrupted or password-protected.\par
\par
Verify the application has write permissions to the 'Raw Report Export' and 'Final Excel Save Location' directories (viewable via Options -> View Configuration).\par
\par
For 'Weekly' reports, ensure the central Power BI source Excel file (typically in your user's Harlow Printing\\IT - Documents\\PowerBI\\... path) is accessible and not locked by another user or process.\par
\par
Email Sending Fails:\par
\par
Check SMTP server settings in appsettings.json (server address, port, username/password if required, SSL setting). These are usually pre-configured by IT.\par
\par
Ensure your network connection allows outgoing SMTP traffic on the configured port.\par
\par
Verify email recipients via Options -> Manage Email Recipients and greetings via Options -> Manage Email Greetings. Invalid email addresses can cause failures.\par
\par
Auto-Run Not Working as Expected:\par
\par
Confirm the main auto-run feature is enabled (button should be green and show 'Disable Daily Auto Run @ %%CURRENT_AUTORUN_HOUR%%:00').\par
\par
Check which specific reports are enabled for auto-run via Options -> Configure Auto-Run Reports.\par
\par
Check the configured 'Auto-Run Hour' via Options -> Set Auto-Run Hour... (currently ~%%CURRENT_AUTORUN_HOUR%%:00). The check happens around this hour.\par
\par
Review appsettings.json for AutoReport:LastRunDate and AutoReport:DailyRunStatus. If LastRunDate is today, or if the specific report's success flag in DailyRunStatus (for today's StatusDate) is true, it generally won't run again until the next due time/day.\par
\par
Incorrect Formulas in Excel Output: The application copies formulas from the first data row (typically row 6) of the 'Analysis' sheet in the template file. If these formulas are incorrect in the template, the output will also be incorrect.\par
\par
User Settings Not Taking Effect (Recipients/Greetings/Bank Holidays): Custom settings are stored in JSON files in your user's AppData folder (e.g., %APPDATA%\\HarlowSolutions\\QuoteConversionReportAutomation\\). If changes aren't applying, check if these files (user_email_settings.json, user_greeting_settings.json, custom_bank_holidays.json) are writable or have become corrupted. Deleting a corrupted user settings file will cause the application to revert to the defaults from appsettings.json for that specific setting type.\par
\par
Excel Slicers/Pivot Tables Not Updating (Manual Refresh Reports): For reports requiring manual refresh (Monthly, Quarterly, Annual, Custom), ensure you follow the on-screen prompts carefully: Open Excel, Enable Editing, go to the Data tab and click Refresh All, then Save and Close Excel before confirming in the application.\par
\par
Check Application Logs: The most detailed error information is usually found in the log files. Access these via Options -> Open Logs Folder. Log files are created daily and are named with the date (e.g., YYYY-MM-DD_LogFile.log).\par
\par
Contact IT Support: If problems persist after checking these steps, please contact IT support with details of the issue and any relevant error messages from the logs.\par
\par
Thank you for using the Quote Conversion Report Automation program!\par
